{
  "image_name": "2d69362c586592840cb01b2cdd5a2860.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure compares two small-RNA pathways that silence repetitive or transposon-rich genomic regions by linking RNA recognition to repressive chromatin. In the upper scheme, repeat-derived transcripts are copied by RdRP into double-stranded RNA, then cut by Dicer into siRNAs. These siRNAs load into a RITS-like complex that binds nascent RNA made by RNA polymerase II and recruits chromatin factors such as SUV39 and HP1. The result is deposition and spreading of H3K9 methylation along the underlying DNA, creating heterochromatin and reinforcing silencing. The diagram also suggests a feedback loop in which repeat transcripts continue to feed siRNA production.\n\nThe lower scheme shows a piRNA-based route centered on Piwi/Aub and Ago3 complexes. Transposon mRNAs are sliced in a cyclic “ping-pong” amplification process, generating additional piRNAs. piRNA cluster transcripts seem to provide precursor molecules that load into Piwi/Aub. These piRNA-guided complexes then target nascent transcripts at chromatin and, again through SUV39, HP1, and H3K9 methylation, establish a repressive state. Overall, the figure emphasizes that both siRNA and piRNA systems use sequence-guided RNA complexes to direct heterochromatin formation and transcriptional gene silencing.",
